# Parity with oxlint-plugin-anti-slop

The originals are Oxlint JavaScript plugins: they run against an ESTree AST with **scope
analysis**, can resolve identifiers to their bindings, walk ancestors, read comments, and hold
per-file state across nodes. Biome plugins are GritQL patterns: **structural matching only**, with
no scope analysis, no type information, no cross-statement dataflow, and no comment attachment.

Several rules therefore cannot be reproduced exactly. Every port was written and then attacked by
a second, independent reviewer whose job was to find false positives and false negatives; what
survived is recorded here. The guiding rule throughout was **prefer a false negative to a false
positive** — a linter that fires on correct code gets switched off, taking its true positives with
it.

Verified against Biome 2.5.8. `test/harness.mjs` pins every claim below: 924 violation cases that
must fire and ~1,900 lines of valid code that must stay silent.

