## Validated against a real codebase

Both linters were run over the same 200-file, 36k-line TypeScript application (a Biome user, not
an Oxlint one) and their findings compared at file:line granularity.

| Rule | oxlint | port | identical | missed by port | extra |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `no-chained-type-assertions` | 18 | 18 | 18 | 0 | 0 |
| `no-conditional-empty-object-spread` | 68 | 68 | 68 | 0 | 0 |
| `no-runtime-typeof` | 86 | 86 | 86 | 0 | 0 |
| `no-shape-in-symbol-names` | 3 | 3 | 3 | 0 | 0 |
| `no-unknown-parameters` | 36 | 36 | 36 | 0 | 0 |
| `no-unknown-returns` | 12 | 12 | 12 | 0 | 0 |
| `no-unsafe-dictionary-type` | 34 | 34 | 33 | 0 | 0 |
| `require-safety-comment-for-type-assertion` | 248 | 248 | 248 | 0 | 0 |
| `no-known-value-widening` | 31 | 27 | 25 | 4 | 0 |
| **total** | **536** | **532** | **529** | **4** | **0** |

**No false positives.** Every finding the port produced was also produced by the original.

Eight of the nine rules that fired reproduced the original exactly. The ninth,
`no-known-value-widening`, finds 27 of 31, with 4 genuinely missed — all needing the scope
analysis described below. The remaining six rules found nothing in either implementation, so they
are unvalidated here rather than confirmed.

Three apparent divergences are span placement, not disagreement. The port anchors a multi-line
annotated declaration at the `const` and an unsafe dictionary at its offending index signature,
where the original anchors at the value and at the enclosing alias. Same findings, different line.

One artefact: `require-safety-comment-for-type-assertion` shows 16 extra findings in a
`routeTree.gen.ts`. Oxlint skips gitignored files by default and that generated file is gitignored;
Biome linted it. On the same file set the rule matches exactly.

### What the real-world run caught that the fixtures did not

Running against real code twice found defects that ~1,900 lines of fixtures never triggered, which
is why `test/robustness.mjs` now exists:

- `no-widen-then-assert` aborted on **any file containing a destructuring declaration**, silently
  reporting nothing for it.
- `no-unknown-type-aliases` was quadratic: 39.8s on 300 type aliases, versus 0.05s now. On a
  generated types file with thousands of aliases, `biome lint` would appear to hang on every save.
- `no-unsafe-dictionary-type` spent 5.6s on the same file, versus 0.04s now.

Both failure modes are silent — an aborted rule looks exactly like a clean codebase, and a
quadratic rule fails as a hang rather than a wrong answer. Neither is detectable from a rule's own
fixtures, so the robustness suite runs every rule against hostile constructs and a scaled file
under a time budget.

## Two limits that apply to every rule

**Diagnostic messages are static.** `register_diagnostic` does not interpolate metavariables — a
message containing `$name` emits the literal text `$name`. Where an original says
``Rename symbol `UserShape`…``, the port says `Rename this symbol…` and points the span at it.

**Suppression is package-wide per line.** All plugin diagnostics share Biome's single `plugin`
category, so `// biome-ignore lint: reason` silences every anti-slop rule on that line.

---

## `no-chained-type-assertions`

**Full parity.** Catches every chain of two or more type assertions in either spelling (`x as T`
and `<T>x`, including mixed chains), through any parenthesis depth and any chain length, reports
once per chain on the outermost assertion, and permits all-`as const` chains without being fooled
by an assertion sitting in an unrelated operand.

This is the rule that pushed hardest on the engine. Angle-bracket assertions were initially
written off as unreachable — no snippet matches them — until the reviewer found that AST node-kind
patterns work (`TsTypeAssertionExpression(ty = $t, expression = $e)`), and that `contains … until`
reproduces the original's parenthesis-unwrapping loop at unbounded depth. Both discoveries are
documented in [GRITQL-NOTES.md](./GRITQL-NOTES.md).

No known gaps.

## `no-conditional-empty-object-spread`

Reports every `{ ...(cond ? { a } : {}) }` the original reports — any parenthesis depth, either
branch empty, any position in any member list, including object literals nested in JSX attributes,
class bodies, defaults, arrays and template literals — and stays quiet where the original does
(casts, calls, member access or logical operators between the spread and the ternary, `({})`
branches, JSX spread attributes, array and call spreads).

- **False positive:** an array-literal or call-argument spread is also reported when its argument
  is *textually identical* to an object spread's argument in an enclosing object literal. GritQL
  has no parent access, so the guard can only compare text. The shape it misfires on throws a
  `TypeError` at runtime and fails type-checking, so it is unlikely to exist in real code.

## `no-known-value-widening`

Catches an explicitly broad target and a syntactically visible known value written **at the same
site**, against `unknown`, `object`, `Record<K,V>`, index-signature and mapped types, anonymous type
literals, and those wrapped in `Readonly` / `Partial` / `Required` / `NonNullable`.

Moving the owner set onto `JsVariableDeclarator` and `JsPropertyClassMember` reached `let`, `var`,
`const`, multi-declarator statements and class properties in one pattern, and switching the return
scan to `contains` reached returns nested inside `if` / `try` / loop blocks. On real code this took
it from 21 of 31 findings to 27 of 31. It also fixed a false positive where the unguarded regex
`r"true|false|null"` matched the identifiers `trueValue`, `maybenull` and `falseAlarm`.

- **Cannot see evidence that arrives through a variable.** The original walks the scope chain to
  the declarator, confirms it is never reassigned, and re-checks its initialiser. This is the
  bulk of what it still misses.
- **Cannot resolve type aliases**, so any broad target named by an alias is missed.
- Generic signatures are unreachable: a metavariable inside a type-parameter list fails to compile.
- **False positive:** `Record`, `Readonly`, `Partial`, `Required` and `NonNullable` are always
  treated as the built-ins. The original skips them when a local import, alias, interface or enum
  shadows the name.

## `no-module-mocking`

Catches Vitest/Jest module mocking (`mock`, `doMock`, `unstable_mockModule`) on `vi` / `jest` and
on the names those packages are imported under, in every call spelling — dotted, string-keyed,
optional-chained, optional-call, type-argumented and parenthesised — and stays silent when the
name is a parameter, catch binding, loop binding, local declaration, or an import from another
module.

- **The shadowing guard is file-scoped, not scope-scoped.** Any `vi` / `jest` binding declared
  anywhere in the file — even inside a nested function where it could not possibly shadow —
  silences that name for the whole file.
- **False positive:** a TypeScript `namespace` or `enum` named `vi` / `jest` is not recognised as
  a binding, so calls on it are reported.
- A parameter list that merely *mentions* the name in a type annotation or default exempts that
  function's body.
- Computed keys are matched by source text, so an escaped spelling of `"mock"` is missed.

## `no-object-parameters`

Catches `object` as a parameter's own top-level type in every owner form — functions, arrows,
methods, constructors (including overload signatures and parameter properties), `declare
function`, function and constructor types, and call / construct / interface method signatures —
including optional, rest, destructured, defaulted, parenthesised, commented and
union-containing-`object` annotations. Correctly ignores `Array<object>`, `{ meta: object }`,
`object[]`, `A & object`, `Map<string | object, number>` and `(object) => void` callbacks.

Rewritten on node kinds (`JsFormalParameter` / `JsRestParameter` / `TsThisParameter` with
`TsNonPrimitiveType`), which fixed three defects at once: it now reports **every** `object`
parameter in a list rather than only the first, reaches **setters**, and no longer aborts on
parameter lists that begin and end with a bracket (`function g({ id }: object, xs: string[])`
previously reported nothing at all).

- Type aliases that resolve to `object` are not followed.
- Parameter decorators are matched by the pattern, but Biome refuses to parse them unless the
  consumer sets `javascript.parser.unsafeParameterDecoratorsEnabled`.

## `no-reflect-apply`

## `no-reflect-get`

Both catch their method written directly on the `Reflect` identifier in all sixteen spellings —
dot or computed string key, with or without optional chaining on the member and on the call, with
or without explicit type arguments — in every expression position including JSX, decorators, class
bodies and template interpolations.

Both collapsed from a 16-branch `or` to a single `JsCallExpression` node-kind pattern with
byte-identical spans, which also closed the parenthesised-callee gaps at unbounded depth.

- **False positive:** Biome has no scope analysis, so a local `const`, parameter, import or catch
  binding named `Reflect` that shadows the global is still flagged. The original resolves the
  identifier and bails.
- Misses a computed key spelling the method name with escape sequences.
- Indirect references (`globalThis.Reflect.apply`, an aliased `const R = Reflect`, a variable
  computed key) are missed by **both** implementations — the original also requires a bare
  `Reflect` identifier.

## `no-runtime-typeof`

Catches every runtime `typeof` expression in any operand shape and any syntactic position, and
correctly ignores TypeScript type queries (`type T = typeof x`, `keyof typeof x`, `x as typeof y`,
generic constraints, `v is typeof x`).

- **The `allowInTypeGuards: true` option cannot be honoured.** Detecting it requires walking up to
  the nearest enclosing function and inspecting its return type for a type predicate; GritQL has
  no ancestor traversal of that kind. The port always reports, which matches the rule's **default**
  configuration exactly but over-reports for anyone who opted in. If you need that option, drop
  this rule and keep the rest.

## `no-shape-in-symbol-names`

Flags the case-insensitive substring `shape` in every identifier the parser gives a node to —
bindings, references, assignment targets, member / private / computed-by-reference names, object,
class and type-member keys, enum members, labels, import/export aliases, import-attribute keys,
namespace, interface, type-alias, type-parameter, `infer`, mapped-type and predicate names, and
JSX element / attribute / namespace names. Correctly leaves strings, template text, regexes,
comments, module specifiers and every quoted key alone.

- Misses an identifier spelled with a Unicode escape.
- An import-attribute key's span covers the whole `key: value` entry, because Biome keeps the key
  as a bare token.
- Shorthand object properties are reported once here and twice by the original (ESTree gives them
  two Identifier nodes over the same range).
- JSX coverage is implemented and was verified against a `.tsx` probe, but has no checked-in
  regression test because the fixture layout is `.ts` only.

## `no-unknown-parameters`

Catches a bare `unknown` annotation (optionally parenthesised) on any parameter — ordinary,
optional, defaulted, destructured, rest, parameter-property and `this` — in every owner the
original visits, including interfaces, type literals, ambient declarations, overload signatures and
function types. Exempts a parameter named exactly `cause`, per the original's convention.

- **False positive:** a `cause` written with a Unicode escape is reported, because the exemption
  matches source text rather than the decoded identifier.
- Decorated parameters and `this` parameters on accessors never reach the plugin — Biome's parser
  rejects both (the latter unconditionally).

## `no-unknown-returns`

Catches every explicitly written `unknown` return contract — plain `unknown`, unions containing it,
parenthesised forms, and `Promise` / `PromiseLike` wrapping any of those to any depth — on every
declaration form: functions, arrows, methods, getters, overloads, `declare` and ambient members,
abstract members, function and constructor types, and interface / type-literal call, construct,
method and getter signatures.

- **Does not resolve type aliases**, so a return type written as a named alias that expands to
  `unknown` is missed: `type UnknownValue = unknown; function load(): UnknownValue`.

## `no-unknown-type-aliases`

Flags any top-level type alias whose right-hand side is `unknown`, including generic aliases and up
to two layers of parentheses, and follows same-file alias chains up to four hops through `export`
and `declare` wrappers. Ignores aliases declared inside any nested body, exactly as the original
does.

- Chains longer than five aliases, and parenthesis nesting three deep or more, are missed. GritQL
  cannot express bounded recursion safely — a recursive named pattern crashes the Biome worker —
  so the chain is unrolled a fixed number of times.
- **False positive:** two top-level aliases sharing one name. The original keeps a map keyed by
  name so only the survivor is tested; the port tests every declaration it sees.

## `no-unsafe-dictionary-type`

Catches every dictionary whose escape-hatch value type is **written out where it is used** —
`Record<K, unknown | any | object | {}>` including unions and transparent wrappers, plus index
signatures and mapped types in type literals and interfaces.

Rewritten on `TsIndexSignatureTypeMember` / `TsIndexSignatureClassMember` / `TsMappedType`, which
replaced two ~700-character regexes tested against every node in the file. That closed two silent
misses — **class index signatures** (`class Bag { [key: string]: unknown }`) and index signatures
whose sibling member nests more than two braces deep — removed the arity cap on intersections, and
took the rule from 5.6s to 0.04s on a 300-declaration file.

- **No alias, generic, or `Pick`/`Omit` resolution.** An escape hatch reached only through a name is
  missed. An unsafe alias is reported at its declaration but not at its use sites.
- Empty interfaces used as a dictionary value need a program-wide interface table and are missed.
- The built-in shadow guard is file-wide and textual, so any local declaration or import of
  `Readonly` / `Partial` / `Required` / `NonNullable` anywhere in a file over-suppresses.
- Nested dictionaries are reported twice where the original suppresses the inner report. Both
  diagnostics land on genuinely unsafe code.

## `no-widen-then-assert`

Flags a `const` that widens a known value to `unknown` / `any` / `object` / a broad `Record` and is
later asserted back to a narrower type, matched by name within the innermost enclosing function,
method, accessor, generator or namespace body — or the module top level — including assertions
inside `if` / `try` / `while` / `switch` blocks and object literals.

The original does real dataflow here. This port approximates it with name matching inside a
textual region, which costs both directions:

- **False positive:** no ordering check. GritQL exposes no source positions, so an assertion
  written *before* its declaration in the same region is still reported.
- Angle-bracket assertions are unreachable; a widened `const` declared inside a nested block,
  object literal, class body or declaring loop is invisible; `for` loops and `catch` clauses are
  hard boundaries.
- Evidence carried by a parameter annotation is invisible, and evidence resolution walks only one
  `const` hop where the original recurses.

## `require-safety-comment-for-type-assertion`

Catches every type assertion except `as const` — both spellings, in expression and
assignment-target positions, in `.ts` and `.tsx`.

The `SAFETY:` exemption used to be **file-granular**: one comment anywhere switched the rule off
for the whole file, so it degraded as it was adopted — the first justification a developer wrote
disabled the rest of the file. It is now scoped to the nearest enclosing region (function body,
statement, or braceless clause body), verified against the oxlint original across 1,416 findings
with zero false positives.

Remaining divergences:

- Suppression is region-granular, not statement-granular: a `SAFETY:` comment still exempts other
  assertions in the same innermost region, and one written *after* an assertion in that region
  still exempts it. Comment-to-node attachment does not exist in GritQL.
- The text is matched in source, not in comments specifically, so a string literal or identifier
  spelled `SAFETY:` exempts its region.
